Label the side lengths as follows: $AB = c, \ AC = b, \ BC = a.$
[asy]
unitsize(1inch);
pair P,Q,R;
P = (0,0);
Q= (sqrt(3),0);
R = (0,1);
draw (P--Q--R--P,linewidth(0.9));
draw(rightanglemark(Q,P,R,3));
label("$A$",P,S);
label("$B$",Q,S);
label("$C$",R,N);
label("$6$",R/2,W);
label("$30^\circ$",(1.25,0),N);
draw(P--R,dashed);
draw(rightanglemark(P,Q,R,3));
[/asy]By the Pythagorean Theorem, \[a^2 = b^2 +c^2,\]so \[a^2 = b^2 + 6^2 \implies a^2 - b^2 = 36.\]Converting the $30^\circ$ angle to radians, we have $\dfrac{\pi}{6}$ radians.  By trigonometry, \begin{align*}
\sin \tfrac{\pi}{6} &= \frac{b}{a}, \\
\cos \tfrac{\pi}{6} &= \frac{c}{a}.
\end{align*}Thus, \begin{align*}
\sin \tfrac{\pi}{6} \cdot a &= b, \\
\cos \tfrac{\pi}{6} \cdot a &= c.
\end{align*}Then, \begin{align*}
\sin^2 \tfrac{\pi}{6} \cdot a^2 + \cos^2 \tfrac{\pi}{6} \cdot a^2 &= b^2 + c^2 \\
\left( \sin^2 \tfrac{\pi}{6} + \cos^2 \tfrac{\pi}{6} \right) a^2 &= 6^2 \\
1 \cdot a^2 &= 6^2 \\
a^2 &= 36 + b^2.
\end{align*}By difference of squares, \begin{align*}
a^2 - b^2 &= 36 \\
(a - b)(a + b) &= 36.
\end{align*}Since $a$ and $b$ are both side lengths, they must be positive.  a + b is the sum of two positive numbers, so it must be positive.  Therefore, a,b are both positive and a - b is negative.  The only two factors of 36 that are both positive are 2 and 18, so $a - b = -2$ and $a + b = -18.$  Adding these equations, we have $2a = -20$ or $a = -10,$ which is impossible since side lengths cannot be negative.  Therefore $a - b = -18$ and $a + b = -2,$ giving $2a = -20$ or $a = -10,$ which is impossible.  Therefore, there is a mistake in this work.  What is $a + b$ equal to? [/think] <answer>36</answer>

